    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>American code breakers had partly read the Japanese naval code and knew the target and the likely date, while the Japanese remained largely unaware of their opponent's strength and positions even after the battle began.</li><li>All four Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aircraft-carrier">fleet carriers</a> present were sunk, three of them within minutes of the dive-bomber attacks that began at 10:22 on 4 June, while the American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torpedo">torpedo</a> squadrons lost most of their aircraft without scoring a hit.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> lost about 3,000 men and many well-trained pilots, and its ability to replace losses in materiel and men became insufficient as American industrial and training capacity grew.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Yorktown, damaged at the Coral Sea a month before and expected to need months of repair, was made battle-ready at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a> in 72 hours, with repair crews still aboard when she sailed.</p></aside>
    </section>

